<h1>Learn to speak the language of music</h1>

<p class="lede">
  Theory Aide is a free, open-source project for understanding what music is
  doing. Plain English first, with the full depth one click away. No
  subscription, no $900 software. The concepts, a dictionary without gatekeeping,
  and real analysis you can watch working.
</p>

<div class="cards">
  <a class="card" href="/concepts/counterpoint/">
    <h3>Concepts</h3>
    <p>One idea at a time, explained plainly, with a live analyzer you can poke.
    First up: counterpoint.</p>
  </a>
  <a class="card" href="/dictionary/">
    <h3>Dictionary</h3>
    <p>Theory jargon in one honest sentence each. The same definitions the
    extension shows as tooltips.</p>
  </a>
  <a class="card" href="/math/">
    <h3>The Math</h3>
    <p>Music is applied mathematics you can hear. See the waveforms, the ratios,
    and why consonance is arithmetic.</p>
  </a>
</div>

<h2>One brain, two homes</h2>
<p>
  Everything on this site runs the same TypeScript analysis engine as the
  <a href="https://github.com/saarsena/theory-aide">Theory Aide extension for
  Ableton Live</a>. The site teaches the ideas; the extension catches them in
  the music you're actually making. Learn it here, hear it there.
</p>
